 ## Overview
-Sub-case decomposition reduces the configuration space explosion in n-dimensional marching cubes by decomposing major cases into edge-connected sub-cases, where vertices share the same status relative to the threshold [S1]. Unlike [Lookup Table and Case Reduction](15794-lookup-table-and-case-reduction.md) which groups cases by symmetry, this method further fragments major cases into simpler geometric primitives (simplices) to manage higher-dimensional complexity, addressing the limitations of sibling methods like [Improved Marching Cubes with New Table and Displacement](14182-improved-marching-cubes-with-new-table-and-displacement.md) which do not explicitly handle the combinatorial growth in dimensions $n > 3$ [S1].
+Sub-case decomposition reduces the configuration space explosion in n-dimensional marching cubes by decomposing complex "major cases" into edge-connected "sub-cases" (fragments of boundary where vertices share the same status relative to the threshold) [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on symmetry grouping alone, this approach stores geometric primitives (simplices) for these smaller fragments, allowing the algorithm to handle higher dimensions (e.g., 4D) where the number of major cases becomes prohibitive [S1]. It sets itself apart by using a primary/secondary table structure where the primary table maps cell configurations to sub-case indices and orientation matrices, rather than storing full triangulations for every symmetric variant [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** An n-dimensional hypercube grid with scalar field values at vertices and a threshold value.
-*   **Output:** A set of simplices (tetrahedra in 3D, hyper-tetrahedra in n-D) representing the isosurface.
+**Inputs:**
+*   An n-dimensional structured grid of scalar values.
+*   An isovalue threshold.
+*   The dimension $n$ of the grid (typically $n \ge 3$).
+
+**Outputs:**
+*   A set of simplices (tetrahedra in 3D, pentachora in 4D, etc.) representing the isosurface.
 
